18. H9 notices come up (H9Vgui)

   A notice (not a status) now comes up over 0.35 s from when its slot began to show; before, it only
   faded out (a hold, then a fade). A banner or flash that pops in rises, as the siege's alarm line does
   every tsm_alarm_every seconds. A notice that replaces one still showing keeps that one's start, so a bar
   sent every tick (the planter's) does not come up again each time; a status shows at once, as before.
   NoticeAlpha still says when a notice is over, NoticeDrawAlpha is what its panel is drawn at, and
   H9NoticeDef gained `shown` (h9_vgui.cpp, h9_vgui.h). The client.dll this replaced is kept as
   client.dll.bak_noticerise in reTS\binaries\ts\cl_dlls\.

19. The laser rifle, weapon 40, and a 15th ammo caliber, plasma cells

   An energy weapon (dlls\ts_weapon_info.cpp, shared with mp.dll). The client has its buy-menu row (Rifles, 5200;
   caliber 15, 35 cells a pack), its weapon-bar cell (column 4, row 12), its picture (sprites\weapons\laserrifle.spr,
   which the buy menu and the select bar both draw) and its fire sound (h9\laserrifle\fire.wav). Only a shot of
   caliber 15 gets the plasma presentation (cl_dll\ev_ts.cpp, ts_bullets.cpp): no brass, no gun smoke, no pellet
   sprite or white streak, no bullet hole or ricochet. It draws a green muzzle flash (sprites\laserrifle_muzzle.spr,
   a frame at random, turned at random) and a faint green dynamic light at the muzzle for every shooter; a green bolt
   that runs from the muzzle to where the shot lands, its trail holding at full brightness a moment behind the head
   and then fading, the head resting at the end (TSPlasma_*, the triangle API in the transparent pass; sprites
   laserrifle_trail, laserrifle_bolt, laserrifle_hit), every ribbon, head and flash kept between a least and a most
   number of pixels across at its own distance, also for a round the bullet simulation flies (stepped every frame,
   with a piece of trail each frame and the last piece laid to the impact); where a shot lands, a scorch decal ({smscorch), a green flash and light and its own
   sound (h9\laserrifle\hit1..3.wav), on a wall or a player; and over the reload an energy cell sound
   (h9\laserrifle\reload.wav, EV_TSReload). Right click zooms as the Barrett (32, then 8) and the scope overlay tints the
   view blue and frames it in one flat blue for this weapon (sprites\laserscope.spr, ts_scope.cpp). SrvSett id 4, the
   server's h9_customweapons, takes the weapons H9 adds (the PPSh-41, the laser rifle) out of the buy menu when the
   server plays TS 3.0's own roster (ts_status.cpp, ts_buymenu.cpp). Detail: reference\DEVIATIONS.md. The client.dll this replaced is kept as
   client.dll.bak_prelaser in reTS\binaries\ts\cl_dlls\.

20. The H9 panels a quarter smaller, and a side's reinforcements on the scoreboard

   The H9 panels (cl_dll\vgui_H9Panel.cpp: the match mode's menus, banners, notices and the match bar) are built at
   three quarters of the size they were: H9_MENU_SCALE 0.68 -> 0.51 and H9_BANNER_SCALE 0.60 -> 0.45, with
   cl_h9_vgui_scale on top as before. Their text comes from three new four-step font ladders in the text scheme files
   (textscheme\*_textscheme.txt: "H9 Text 1-4", "H9 Detail 1-4", "H9 Flash 1-4", three quarters of the stock scheme at
   the same step, never under 10 px); a scheme file without them gets the stock schemes at the same steps. H9Pool (byte
   side, short left, short start) is the new user message of a mode that keeps one pool of lives for a whole side (the assault mode,
   which calls it REINFORCEMENTS): the scoreboard's team rows show the pool left out of the pool the round started with in
   the lives column, which is then titled REINFORCEMENTS (REINFORCE or REINF where the names would lose more than 40% of
   their width, PlaceCells in vgui_ScorePanel.cpp), and the players' rows show nothing there (ts_matchmode sends each player's H9Lives as 0 then). Detail: docs\H9_VGUI.txt.

21. Marks: points of the world drawn through the walls (H9Vgui op MARK, protocol 3)

   The H9Vgui message has a new op, MARK (22), and _h9vgui now says 3. A mark is a point of the world a mode wants every
   player to know of (the assault mode's capture points): a slot 1-32, its owner (0, 1 or 2; 3 takes the slot down and
   slot 0 takes every mark down), its state (quiet, being taken, contested), the side taking it, how far that has got, its
   x, y, z and a label of up to 7 characters. cl_dll\h9_vgui.cpp draws each one every HUD frame where the point lands on
   the screen (pTriAPI->WorldToScreen), so through any wall: a badge framed in the owner's colour (blue for side 1, red
   for side 2, grey for nobody, yellow and the owner's by turns while contested) round a dark fill, the label in white
   (the HUD font is drawn additively, so it only shows over a dark ground), with a bar under it while a side is taking the
   point; a point out of view is pinned to the edge of the screen in its direction. With two marks or more the same
   badges also stand in a row under the top stack of notices, and TS's own notices go on beneath the row
   (H9Vgui_StackBottom). A mark that nothing refreshes goes after 30 s, a new map or server clears every one, and they
   hide with the HUD and under the scoreboard. cl_h9_marks and cl_h9_marks_strip (both 1, saved) turn the marks and the
   row off. A game of protocol 2 or less is sent TS's own Objective markers instead. Detail: docs\H9_VGUI.txt.

22. Marks: a badge as wide as its label, room between the badges, and tsm_show_zones

   A mark's badge was always a square, so a word such as UPLINK was wider than its frame and ran into the badge beside it
   in the row under the top stack ("4UPLINK"). cl_dll\h9_vgui.cpp now gives each badge the width its label needs
   (BadgeWide: the square, or the text plus room either side), on the world and in the row, and the row's badges stand
   a few pixels apart frame to frame (StripGap) where they used to touch; a wide badge pinned to the edge of the screen
   keeps its whole frame on the screen. tsm_show_zones (1, saved) is the new client cvar for the marks drawn on the world,
   through the walls: 0 draws none there (the assault mode's capture points, the uplink mode's zones and uplink, the flags
   and pads of Capture the Flag) while the row under the top stack stays (cl_h9_marks_strip), and cl_h9_marks still turns
   every mark off. The client.dll this replaced is kept as client.dll.bak_prezones in reTS\binaries\ts\cl_dlls\.

23. Scoreboard: a class column

   H9Class (ts_matchmode: byte slot, byte class-icon frame + 1; slot 0 says the column is off) names each player's class icon
   as a frame of sprites/tsm_class.spr, the sheet of the icons over players' heads. cl_dll\vgui_ScorePanel.cpp reads that sheet
   once (an IDSP version 2 sprite, 8-bit palette, index 255 clear when alpha-tested) and draws the frame at 16 x 16 in a new
   cell, SB_CELL_CLASS, between the name and the kills; the cell has no width on a server that sends no H9Class. The server
   decides who may see whose class (tsm_competitive), so a player it did not name shows none. The client.dll this replaced is
   kept as client.dll.bak_preclass in reTS\binaries\ts\cl_dlls\.

24. cl_h9_show_reinforce: hide the respawn wave countdown

   cl_h9_show_reinforce (1, saved) is the client cvar for the line that counts down the side's next respawn wave, the notice the
   server sends under the slot name tsm.wave (the Officer's always, and with the server's tsm_show_reinforce every living player's).
   cl_dll\h9_vgui.cpp's StoreNotice clears that slot instead of storing the notice while the cvar is 0. The client.dll this
   replaced is kept as client.dll.bak_prereinf in reTS\binaries\ts\cl_dlls\.

25. Everything else reTS already had

   The reTS client is a recompilation of the whole TS client, so it also carries the third-person
   self-transparency fade, the predicted viewmodel body, the HUD and the rest of the reTS client
   work -- none of that is a patch on top of the stock DLL.

Building and deploying
----------------------

    cd reTS
    $env:RETS_REBUILD = '1'
    .\scripts\build-win32-dropin.ps1

writes binaries\ts\cl_dlls\client.dll (and the server's mp.dll). xash.bat's assemble step sets
RETS_REBUILD=1, so the H9XASH package takes this pair. The GoldSrc package does not yet: while
reTS\binaries\goldsrc\ holds a pair, export.bat's GoldSrc block copies that one into export\ts\,
the last pair built for 32 player slots (client.dll 895488 bytes, SHA256
92E1BFF2614EA881692CDEA86BAF405AD95D3F70BDBDE19BD958C8E0A5267E97), and the Steam install keeps it.
This pair goes there once it has passed the regression tests at 32 (docs\PLAYER_LIMIT_128.txt,
section 23) and reTS\binaries\goldsrc\ is removed.

The older stock client with the binary cloak patch (865280 bytes, MD5
74fc8b3039032a31f7d81440bbde0e80) is kept in
backups\goldsrc_source_build_20261029\cl_dlls\client.dll and decompile_dll\client_patched_cloak.dll;
the appendix describes that build.

